## 5. Model Architecture

### 5.1 Base Model: multilingual-e5-small

```
Architecture:   XLM-RoBERTa (transformer encoder)
Parameters:     ~117 million
Embedding dim:  384
Max seq len:    512 tokens
Tokeniser:      SentencePiece (256k vocabulary, Devanagari-aware)
Languages:      100+  (including Sanskrit, Hindi, all Indic scripts)
Pooling:        Mean pooling over last hidden states
Normalisation:  L2 norm → unit vectors on the embedding hypersphere
```

### 5.2 Why This Model

| Criterion | multilingual-e5-small | bge-small-en | gte-multilingual |
|---|---|---|---|
| Sanskrit support | ✅ Native Devanagari | ❌ English only | ✅ Multilingual |
| VRAM (inference) | ~0.5 GB | ~0.5 GB | ~1.0 GB |
| Embedding dim | 384 | 384 | 768 |
| Retrieval quality | High | Very High (EN) | Very High |
| Fine-tune time (T4) | ~8 min | ~8 min | ~15 min |
| **Best for Sanskrit** | **✅ Yes** | ❌ No | ✅ Yes |

**Decision:** `multilingual-e5-small` is the minimum-viable choice that handles Devanagari Unicode natively while fitting comfortably on a free-tier Colab T4.

### 5.3 Embedding Computation

```
Input text
    │
    ▼
SentencePiece Tokeniser
(handles Devanagari sub-word units)
    │
    ▼
12-Layer XLM-RoBERTa Encoder
[token_1, token_2, ..., token_n]  →  [h_1, h_2, ..., h_n]
    │
    ▼
Mean Pooling:  e = (1/n) Σ h_i
    │
    ▼
L2 Normalisation:  ê = e / ||e||₂
    │
    ▼
384-dimensional unit vector  ê ∈ ℝ³⁸⁴,  ||ê|| = 1
```

Cosine similarity reduces to dot product for normalised vectors:
```
sim(q, d) = q · d  =  cos(θ)  ∈ [-1, 1]
```

---

## 6. Training Methodology

### 6.1 MultipleNegativesRankingLoss (Primary)

**Mathematical formulation:**

For a batch of N (query, positive) pairs:

```
L_MNRL = -(1/N) Σᵢ log [ exp(sim(qᵢ, pᵢ) · τ⁻¹) /
                          Σⱼ exp(sim(qᵢ, pⱼ) · τ⁻¹) ]

where:
  sim(a, b) = a · b     (cosine sim, vectors L2-normalised)
  τ = 1/scale = 1/20 = 0.05   (temperature)
  i = query index
  j = all positive documents in batch (j=i is the true positive)
```

This is the **InfoNCE (Noise Contrastive Estimation)** objective, equivalent to a softmax cross-entropy classification where:
- The correct class is the true positive document
- All other batch documents are treated as negatives
- Temperature τ sharpens (τ < 1) or smooths (τ > 1) the distribution

**Effective negatives per step:** `batch_size − 1 = 15`

**Why scale=20 (τ=0.05)?**
- Low temperature creates a peakier distribution → harder optimisation signal
- Forces the model to rank the true positive much higher than all negatives
- Standard in state-of-the-art retrieval models (E5, GTE, BGE all use τ ≈ 0.01–0.07)

### 6.2 TripletLoss (Ablation)

```
L_triplet = max(0,  d(a,p) − d(a,n) + margin)

where:
  d(x,y) = 1 − cos_sim(x,y)    (cosine distance)
  a = anchor (query)
  p = positive document
  n = negative document (explicit hard negative)
  margin = 0.5
```

The margin forces the positive to be at least 0.5 cosine distance **closer** than the negative. Unlike MNRL, TripletLoss uses only **1 explicit negative per step**.

### 6.3 Training Configuration

| Hyperparameter | MNRL | TripletLoss |
|---|---|---|
| Epochs | 3 | 2 |
| Batch size | 16 | 16 |
| Learning rate | 2e-5 | 2e-5 |
| Optimiser | AdamW | AdamW |
| Warmup ratio | 10% | 10% |
| LR schedule | Linear decay | Linear decay |
| Scale (temperature⁻¹) | 20.0 | N/A |
| Triplet margin | N/A | 0.5 |

### 6.4 Training Loop

```python
# Simplified training loop (SentenceTransformers handles internals)

for epoch in range(epochs):
    for batch in dataloader:                    # batch of (query, positive) pairs
        q_emb  = model.encode(batch.queries)    # [B, 384]
        p_emb  = model.encode(batch.positives)  # [B, 384]
        
        # Similarity matrix [B × B]
        sim_matrix = q_emb @ p_emb.T * scale    # scale = 20
        
        # Labels: diagonal is correct (query_i → positive_i)
        labels = torch.arange(B)
        
        # Cross-entropy loss
        loss = cross_entropy(sim_matrix, labels)
        
        loss.backward()
        optimiser.step()
        scheduler.step()
        optimiser.zero_grad()
```

### 6.5 Evaluation During Training

`InformationRetrievalEvaluator` runs every `total_steps // (epochs × 2)` steps and saves the best model checkpoint based on nDCG@10. This prevents overfitting on a small corpus.

---

## 7. Evaluation Framework

### 7.1 Metric Definitions

**Recall@K:**
```
Recall@K = |{relevant docs} ∩ {top-K retrieved}| / |{relevant docs}|
```
Primary metric for RAG systems — measures whether the context needed to answer a query was retrieved.

**Precision@K:**
```
Precision@K = |{relevant docs} ∩ {top-K retrieved}| / K
```
Measures retrieval quality — fraction of retrieved docs that are relevant.

**MRR@K (Mean Reciprocal Rank):**
```
MRR@K = (1/|Q|) Σ_q  1 / rank_q

where rank_q = position of first relevant doc for query q (up to K)
```
Measures how highly the correct answer is ranked. MRR@1 = Recall@1 for single-relevant-doc evaluation.

**nDCG@K (Normalised Discounted Cumulative Gain):**
```
DCG@K  = Σ_{i=1}^{K}  rel_i / log₂(i+1)
IDCG@K = Σ_{i=1}^{min(|R|,K)}  1 / log₂(i+1)     (ideal ranking)
nDCG@K = DCG@K / IDCG@K
```
Rewards finding the correct answer at rank 1 more than rank 5. Normalised to [0, 1].

### 7.2 Evaluation Pipeline

```
1. Encode all corpus documents → [N_docs × 384] matrix
2. Build FAISS IndexFlatIP (exact inner product search)
3. Encode all evaluation queries → [N_queries × 384] matrix
4. Search index: retrieve top-10 per query → scores + indices
5. Compare retrieved indices against relevant_docs ground truth
6. Compute Recall@K, Precision@K, MRR@K, nDCG@K for K ∈ {1,3,5,10}
```

### 7.3 Ground Truth Construction

Each evaluation query has exactly one relevant document (the verse from which the query was generated). This is a standard **single-relevant-document** setup appropriate for the corpus size.

**Limitation:** Multiple verses can correctly answer some queries (e.g. "What is the soul?"). Multi-label ground truth with MAP / R-Precision would be more complete for a production evaluation.

---

## 8. Retrieval Pipeline (Mini-RAG)

### 8.1 Architecture

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                    QUERY PROCESSING                         │
│                                                             │
│  User query  →  Encode (fine-tuned model)  →  384-d vector │
└──────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────┘
                           │
                           ▼
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                    FAISS INDEX                              │
│                                                             │
│  44 verse embeddings  →  IndexFlatIP  (exact cosine search) │
│  Query time: ~2 ms on T4 GPU                                │
└──────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────┘
                           │
                           ▼
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                TOP-K RETRIEVAL                              │
│                                                             │
│  Top-3 (verse_id, cosine_score) pairs returned              │
└──────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────┘
                           │
                           ▼
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                CONTEXT FORMATTING                           │
│                                                             │
│  For each retrieved verse:                                  │
│    • Sanskrit Devanagari text                               │
│    • Roman transliteration                                  │
│    • English translation                                    │
│    • Chapter, verse number, retrieval score                 │
│    • Themes / topic tags                                    │
└──────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────┘
                           │
                           ▼
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│              [→ LLM GENERATION LAYER]                       │
│                                                             │
│  Context passed to LLM (LLaMA-3, Mistral, etc.)             │
│  for grounded answer generation.                            │
│  (Not implemented — retrieval demo only)                    │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

### 8.2 Multilingual Query Support

The system handles queries in multiple input modalities without any language detection or routing logic — the embedding model handles cross-lingual alignment natively:

```python
# All of these correctly retrieve BG_2_47 (karma verse)
rag.query("What does the Gita say about karma?")       # English
rag.query("कर्म का सिद्धांत क्या है?")                    # Hindi
rag.query("karmanye vadhikaraste ma phaleshu")          # Transliteration
rag.query("nishkama karma")                             # Sanskrit keywords
rag.query("How to act without desire for reward?")     # Paraphrase
```

### 8.3 FAISS Index Choice

| Index Type | When to Use | This Project |
|---|---|---|
| `IndexFlatIP` | < 10,000 vectors, exact results | ✅ Used (44 vectors) |
| `IndexIVFFlat` | 10,000–1M vectors, ~99% recall | For scaled version |
| `IndexHNSW` | > 1M vectors, fastest approximate | Production system |

For 44 corpus vectors, `IndexFlatIP` guarantees **perfect index recall** with negligible latency (~0.1 ms).

